+add the audio-fade command and read-only audio-fade property for sample-clocked output fades
diff --git a/DOCS/man/input.rst b/DOCS/man/input.rst
index 70c3418..7ec9a78 100644
--- a/DOCS/man/input.rst
+++ b/DOCS/man/input.rst
@@ -297,6 +297,20 @@ Remember to quote string arguments in input.conf (see `Flat command syntax`_).
 Playback Control
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 
+``audio-fade <target> <duration>``
+    Fade PCM audio in the output mixer, independently of ``volume`` and ``mute``.
+    ``target`` is a linear amplitude multiplier between 0 and 1. ``duration`` is
+    the full-range ramp time in seconds, from 0 to 60; reversing a partial ramp
+    takes proportionally less time. Zero changes the envelope immediately for
+    samples not yet sent to the device. Samples already queued cannot be changed.
+
+    Ramps advance once per output sample frame, not by wall-clock timers or media
+    timestamps. Pausing or starving the audio output does not consume the ramp.
+    A new command replaces the previous ramp at its current render-side gain.
+    The target persists across device recreation; the initial gain is 1.
+    Without an audio output, the command stores its target without a ramp.
+    Bitstream, DSD and 64-bit integer outputs do not support this command.
+
 ``seek <target> [<flags>]``
     Change the playback position. By default, seeks by a relative amount of
     seconds.
@@ -2423,6 +2437,26 @@ Property list
     ``time-remaining/full``
         ``time-remaining`` with milliseconds.
 
+``audio-fade``
+    Output-mixer fade state, as a map with these sub-properties:
+
+    ``gain``
+        Current render-side linear multiplier, between 0 and 1.
+    ``target``
+        Most recently requested multiplier.
+    ``active``
+        True while the envelope is rendering or its last ramp sample is still
+        queued to the audio device. Transport operations such as pause should
+        wait for this to become false, not just for ``gain`` to reach ``target``.
+        The device's reported output delay determines the queued portion.
+    ``supported``
+        True when the current audio output supports fading. False for no output,
+        bitstream/DSD output and unsupported PCM formats.
+
+    A seek discards the queued-completion timestamp but preserves the current
+    envelope. Device recreation starts at the last requested target. Property
+    observers update at the same frequency as ``audio-pts``.
+
 ``audio-pts``
     Current audio playback position in current file in seconds. Unlike ``time-pos``,
     this updates more often than once per frame. This is mostly equivalent to
